  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A detached chain is the same as a lazy daisy petal but understood on its own. You bring the thread up, form a loop, insert the needle back at the starting hole, and bring it up at the tip of the loop. One small "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "tacking-stitch"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "tacking stitch"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " closes the tip. The loop lies flat and holds its shape without needing neighbouring stitches to support it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "Thread",
              "value": "Stranded cotton, 2 to 3 strands"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fabric",
              "value": "Cotton or linen"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needle",
              "value": "Embroidery needle size 9"
            },
            {
              "label": "Hoop",
              "value": "15 cm embroidery hoop"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Working the stitch"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring the needle up at point A. Hold the thread in a loop with your thumb."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Insert the needle back at A (or just beside it) and bring it up at point B, which is the tip of the loop. The thread lies under the needle tip."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pull through gently to form the loop. Do not pull tight. The loop should be rounded."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make one small tacking stitch over the tip of the loop, inserting the needle just beyond B. This anchors the chain."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common mistakes"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "When detached chains go wrong",
          "intro": "Two common problems.",
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The loop puckers and loses its oval shape",
              "cause": "The thread was pulled too tight before the tacking stitch.",
              "fix": "Pull only until the loop lies smoothly on the fabric, then make the tacking stitch while it is still relaxed."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The tacking stitch shows as a visible bar",
              "cause": "Tacking stitch is too long.",
              "fix": "Keep the tacking stitch just 1 to 2 mm, enough to hold but not long enough to be noticeable."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What it underpins"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Detached chains are arranged in groups to form petals (lazy daisy flowers), scatter fills, and leaf pairs. They also appear as individual accent stitches in crewelwork and folk embroidery."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
